bevy-horizon: thread jumps and drop branches to the next block before Volta (NAK_DEBUG=jthr)

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at 606441c9, after 0001-0023. scripts/build_nvk.sh applies it; this text before the first
"diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

Before Volta, an if whose threads may split pushes a reconvergence point with SSY and ends each
side with SYNC. With NAK_DEBUG=ubra (0006), an if whose condition is the same in every thread
uses plain BRAs instead. from_nir ends every block with a jump, though. From Volta on,
opt_jump_thread then sends jumps past blocks that only branch on and drops branches to the
next block; before Volta it never runs, because it can't move a SYNC, BRK or CONT. So with ubra
the world shader of sk8, a Skate 3 port, keeps 35 BRAs to the next block and 23 blocks that only
branch on. On Maxwell each control op holds its warp 13 cycles, and NAK puts a 2-cycle NOP
after it.

With NAK_DEBUG=jthr, jump threading runs before Volta too, after opt_crs. Only a block that
holds just a BRA or an EXIT is jumped past, and only BRAs are retargeted. SYNC, BRK and CONT keep
their targets, and rewrite_cfg keeps their edges, so the CRS stack is pushed and popped as
before and every SSY, PBK and PCNT target stays. from_nir emits a plain BRA only to a block on no
CRS entry, so a BRA never leaves an SSY or PBK region, and neither does a chain of them. Then,
whether or not anything was threaded, branches to the next block are dropped, as Mesa's older
Maxwell compiler does (CodeEmitter::prepareEmission() in nv50_ir_target.cpp).

Compiled on the host with the Switch flags and ubra, sk8's world fragment shaders lose 30-120
instructions each (2,020 to 1,900 for the one with every feature), its water 4,242 to 4,040, and
its compute kernels 14-42; registers, warps and spills don't change. In the world shader with
feature mask 254, control ops go from 119 to 70 (153 without ubra); the 166 fewer instructions
than without either flag are those ops and their NOPs. Without ubra, jthr drops 1-6 branches per
shader.

It is off by default, in Switch builds too, until a run on a Switch shows it is faster.

